\insrsid14158252\charrsid14158252  You pinch a rigid pen loosely at about one-third of its length and waggle your hand up and down.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\kerning2\insrsid14158252\charrsid14158252 \loch\af3\dbch\af31506\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid14158252\charrsid14158252 The Physics:}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14158252\charrsid14158252  Your fingers act as a loose, flexible pivot point (a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid14158252\charrsid14158252 node}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid14158252\charrsid14158252  of minimal displacement). The ends of the pen whip back and forth dynamically (the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid14158252\charrsid14158252 antinodes}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid14158252\charrsid14158252 ). Because the human eye cannot process the rapid changes in direction at the tips, your brain smooths out the motion. This creates the optical illusion that the rigid plastic is bending like rubber. [}
